<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hecatomnid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 395–c. 326 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hecatomnids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hecatomnid dynasty ruled Caria in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> of the Persian Empire in the 4th century BCE. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatomnus">Hecatomnus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Mylasa</a>, it reached its height under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a>, who moved the capital to Halicarnassus and extended Carian power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and other islands; his tomb, the Mausoleum, gave its name to all such buildings. Ada, the last of the line, was confirmed in Caria by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest known member of the family was Hyssaldomus, dynast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Mylasa</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatomnus">Hecatomnus</a> and of a woman named Aba. Louis Robert suggested he was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of Caria, but there is no conclusive evidence. The Hecatomnids were one of many minor Carian dynasties, and the names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pixodarus">Pixodarus</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> records among the dynasts of Cindye during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>, suggest the family ruled that town before moving to Mylasa. Caria had been governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> until Tissaphernes was executed in 395 BCE; Hecatomnus, its first Carian satrap, was in office by 392 BCE, when he made war on Evagoras of Salamis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatomnus">Hecatomnus</a>' five children formed two married pairs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> with Artemisia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrieus">Idrieus</a> with Ada, while the youngest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pixodarus">Pixodarus</a>, married outside the family; since neither pair is known to have had children, their marriages are thought to have been symbolic. Only men were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> or struck coins, but Hecatomnid women issued laws and led military actions, and each widow ruled alone. Mausolus and Artemisia II succeeded in 377 BCE and took Lycia after the Revolt of the Satraps. After Mausolus died in 353/2 BCE, Artemisia suppressed a revolt of the Rhodians, leading a fleet to the island herself.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrieus">Idrieus</a> and Ada I followed about 351/0 BCE, and Idrieus built more at the sanctuary of Labraunda, the centre of the cult of Zeus Labraundeus, than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> had. Ada ruled alone from about 344/3 BCE until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pixodarus">Pixodarus</a> deposed her about 341/0 BCE and exiled her to Alinda. Pixodarus issued the Letoon trilingual in Greek, Lycian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and was the only Hecatomnid to strike gold coins. On his death in 336/5 BCE the Persian Orontobates, husband of his daughter Ada II, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> reached Caria after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Battle of the Granicus</a> in 334 BCE, Ada I, by tradition, came to him from Alinda and adopted him as her son. He ousted Orontobates during the Siege of Halicarnassus, in which Ada took a leading part, and left her to rule Caria from Halicarnassus. She then disappears from the record and may have died at any time between 334 and 323 BCE; after Alexander's death the Macedonian Asander became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>. The dynasty's best-known monument is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausoleum-at-halicarnassus">Mausoleum at Halicarnassus</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hecatomnids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Miletus revolted from Athens in 412 BCE and became a Spartan base, and under the King's Peace of 387/386 BCE it returned to Persian rule, later under the <b>Hecatomnid</b> satraps of Caria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
